WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.211
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Proficiency.

2
00:00:05.511 --> 00:00:08.386
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.686 --> 00:00:18.456
Unit fifty-two: Technology, Power and Control. In this unit, you will report and evaluate arguments about tech power.

4
00:00:19.450 --> 00:00:20.985
digital sovereignty.

5
00:00:21.685 --> 00:00:28.108
digital sovereignty means a country's control over its own data, networks and digital rules.

6
00:00:28.408 --> 00:00:31.696
Europe talks increasingly about digital sovereignty.

7
00:00:32.396 --> 00:00:35.954
Your turn. Say: digital sovereignty.

8
00:00:38.654 --> 00:00:40.062
digital sovereignty.

9
00:00:41.050 --> 00:00:42.283
platform power.

10
00:00:42.983 --> 00:00:50.579
platform power means the influence that big online platforms have over users, markets and information.

11
00:00:50.879 --> 00:00:54.399
Platform power lets a few companies shape what billions see.

12
00:00:55.099 --> 00:00:58.518
Your turn. Say: platform power.

13
00:01:01.218 --> 00:01:02.486
platform power.

14
00:01:03.483 --> 00:01:04.775
tech regulation.

15
00:01:05.475 --> 00:01:10.748
tech regulation means laws controlling how technology companies operate.

16
00:01:11.048 --> 00:01:14.244
Tech regulation struggles to keep up with innovation.

17
00:01:14.944 --> 00:01:18.351
Your turn. Say: tech regulation.

18
00:01:21.051 --> 00:01:22.237
tech regulation.

19
00:01:23.250 --> 00:01:24.750
data colonialism.

20
00:01:25.450 --> 00:01:33.185
data colonialism means the idea that powerful firms extract data from people as colonisers once extracted resources.

21
00:01:33.485 --> 00:01:38.016
Critics describe free apps in poor countries as data colonialism.

22
00:01:38.716 --> 00:01:42.041
Your turn. Say: data colonialism.

23
00:01:44.741 --> 00:01:46.393
data colonialism.

24
00:01:47.383 --> 00:01:48.930
algorithmic governance.

25
00:01:49.630 --> 00:01:54.544
algorithmic governance means using algorithms to make or guide public decisions.

26
00:01:54.844 --> 00:01:59.119
Algorithmic governance decides who receives some welfare payments.

27
00:01:59.819 --> 00:02:03.354
Your turn. Say: algorithmic governance.

28
00:02:06.054 --> 00:02:07.867
algorithmic governance.

29
00:02:08.883 --> 00:02:10.593
digital rights movement.

30
00:02:11.293 --> 00:02:17.971
digital rights movement means activists campaigning for privacy, free expression and fair access online.

31
00:02:18.271 --> 00:02:21.768
The digital rights movement opposed mass surveillance.

32
00:02:22.468 --> 00:02:26.224
Your turn. Say: digital rights movement.

33
00:02:28.924 --> 00:02:30.470
digital rights movement.

34
00:02:31.483 --> 00:02:33.053
open-source movement.

35
00:02:33.753 --> 00:02:40.513
open-source movement means the movement to share software code freely so anyone can use and improve it.

36
00:02:40.813 --> 00:02:43.439
Linux grew out of the open-source movement.

37
00:02:44.139 --> 00:02:47.407
Your turn. Say: open-source movement.

38
00:02:50.107 --> 00:02:51.561
open-source movement.

39
00:02:52.550 --> 00:02:53.714
tech monopoly.

40
00:02:54.414 --> 00:03:00.720
tech monopoly means a technology company that dominates its market without real competition.

41
00:03:01.020 --> 00:03:04.135
Regulators accused the firm of being a tech monopoly.

42
00:03:04.835 --> 00:03:07.858
Your turn. Say: tech monopoly.

43
00:03:10.558 --> 00:03:11.687
tech monopoly.

44
00:03:12.683 --> 00:03:14.996
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:03:15.296 --> 00:03:18.167
Rithy, who really controls the internet?

46
00:03:19.450 --> 00:03:25.028
Critics argue that a handful of firms do. Some even describe it as data colonialism.

47
00:03:26.317 --> 00:03:29.457
That's a strong term. Do the companies accept it?

48
00:03:30.750 --> 00:03:35.457
Not at all. They maintain that their services are free and empower users.

49
00:03:36.750 --> 00:03:38.099
And governments?

50
00:03:39.383 --> 00:03:43.775
The EU has asserted its digital sovereignty through tough tech regulation.

51
00:03:45.083 --> 00:03:46.700
What do activists say?

52
00:03:47.983 --> 00:03:53.315
The digital rights movement warns that algorithmic governance can quietly deny people their rights.

53
00:03:54.617 --> 00:03:59.043
Grammar focus: Precise reporting and attribution (advanced).

54
00:03:59.343 --> 00:04:02.109
Precise attribution keeps reporting fair.

55
00:04:02.409 --> 00:04:08.764
Critics argue that a few firms control the internet. The companies maintain that their services empower users.

56
00:04:09.064 --> 00:04:13.525
Activists warn that algorithmic governance can deny people their rights.

57
00:04:13.825 --> 00:04:22.257
In balanced reporting, attribute each view to its source and choose verbs that reflect the source's strength of claim.

58
00:04:23.250 --> 00:04:26.634
Now read and listen. Who Governs the Digital World?.

59
00:04:26.934 --> 00:04:33.612
A small number of technology firms now shape how billions of people communicate, shop and learn.

60
00:04:34.917 --> 00:04:39.203
Critics argue that this platform power rivals that of many governments.

61
00:04:40.517 --> 00:04:45.790
Some scholars even describe the extraction of personal data as data colonialism.

62
00:04:47.083 --> 00:04:52.345
The companies, for their part, maintain that their services are free, innovative and empowering.

63
00:04:53.650 --> 00:05:01.571
Governments have responded with tech regulation, and the European Union in particular has asserted its digital sovereignty.

64
00:05:02.883 --> 00:05:09.782
Meanwhile, the digital rights movement warns that algorithmic governance may make important decisions without transparency.

65
00:05:11.083 --> 00:05:16.786
The open-source movement offers an alternative model, in which software is shared rather than owned.

66
00:05:18.083 --> 00:05:24.727
Whether regulation can restrain a tech monopoly without stifling innovation remains fiercely debated.

67
00:05:26.017 --> 00:05:28.797
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:05:29.097 --> 00:05:32.942
A country's control over its own data and networks is blank.

69
00:05:33.242 --> 00:05:38.103
A, digital sovereignty. B, platform power. C, tech monopoly.

70
00:05:42.117 --> 00:05:44.964
The answer is A: digital sovereignty.

71
00:05:46.450 --> 00:05:50.087
Which reporting verb shows someone keeps defending a position?

72
00:05:50.387 --> 00:05:53.819
A, maintain. B, admit. C, wonder.

73
00:05:57.817 --> 00:06:00.373
The answer is A: maintain.

74
00:06:01.883 --> 00:06:05.694
'Activists warn that...' presents the activists' view as blank.

75
00:06:05.994 --> 00:06:11.691
A, a caution about danger. B, a joke. C, a proven fact.

76
00:06:15.683 --> 00:06:18.821
The answer is A: a caution about danger.

77
00:06:20.317 --> 00:06:24.209
Sharing software code freely is the idea behind the blank.

78
00:06:24.509 --> 00:06:30.472
A, open-source movement. B, data colonialism. C, algorithmic governance.

79
00:06:34.483 --> 00:06:37.075
The answer is A: open-source movement.

80
00:06:38.583 --> 00:06:42.742
According to the text, which body has asserted its digital sovereignty?

81
00:06:43.042 --> 00:06:47.694
A, the European Union. B, ASEAN. C, the United Nations.

82
00:06:51.683 --> 00:06:54.658
The answer is A: the European Union.

83
00:06:56.150 --> 00:07:02.622
Now it's your turn. Part one: Should Cambodia regulate big technology companies more strictly?

84
00:07:07.922 --> 00:07:14.554
Part two: Report at least two opposing views with precise reporting verbs before giving yours.

85
00:07:20.854 --> 00:07:33.285
Critics argue that foreign platforms collect too much data. The companies maintain that they bring free services. Experts warn that weak regulation leaves users unprotected, so I believe sensible rules are needed.

86
00:07:33.585 --> 00:07:36.513
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:07:37.517 --> 00:07:44.267
Great work today! You learned eight new words and precise reporting and attribution (advanced).

88
00:07:44.567 --> 00:07:49.902
Today's value is online safety. Keep your password secret and be kind online.

89
00:07:50.202 --> 00:07:53.899
See you in Unit fifty-three. Goodbye!

90
00:07:54.199 --> 00:07:56.932
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

